x86: require Harmony guest interfaces without a boot opt-in

The shipped x86 kernel executes only inside Harmony. Enable the deterministic
clock, entropy timing, execution ticks, flags and XSAVE normalization, and
userspace counter confinement from the compiled guest configuration rather
than a kernel command-line token. Stop boot if the required host clock page or
ABI cannot be registered. Keep CONFIG_HARMONY_USER_COUNTER_TRAPS as the
explicit N6 negative-control build profile.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

--- a/arch/x86/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/x86/Kconfig
@@ -840,19 +840,17 @@
 	  Clocksource and sched_clock backed by the harmony deterministic
 	  hypervisor's materialized exit-count-derived clock page (a seqlock page
 	  the host re-stamps at every virtual-time advance; the read path is
-	  a page load, never a raw counter read). Inert unless the
-	  harmony_pvclock kernel parameter is present AND the host accepts
-	  the page registration over the hypercall doorbell; when active the
-	  TSC is marked unstable so kernel timekeeping can never fall back
-	  to raw RDTSC. Say N unless building the harmony determinism guest.
+	  a page load, never a raw counter read). Registration over the
+	  hypercall doorbell is required at boot; a missing or incompatible
+	  host interface stops the kernel. Say Y for the Harmony guest.
 
 config HARMONY_USER_COUNTER_TRAPS
 	bool "Harmony virtualize ring-3 TSC reads"
 	depends on HARMONY_PVCLOCK
 	default y
 	help
-	  On an active harmony_pvclock boot, set CR4.TSD for every newly
-	  executed userspace image and refuse a subsequent PR_TSC_ENABLE.
+	  Set CR4.TSD for every newly executed userspace image and refuse
+	  a subsequent PR_TSC_ENABLE.
 	  Harmony's #GP handler serves those reads from the virtual clock.
 	  Disable only for the N6 negative control kernel that proves the
 	  counter-reading probe detects a live TSC.
